WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.170
A service handles a continuing stream of requests.

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:07.163
Scaling is therefore more than choosing an efficient
algorithm.

00:00:07.163 --> 00:00:11.600
We must understand concurrent demand, throughput,
latency and failure.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.939
Use the course-registration opening rush as a
concrete workload, and ask what performance the
system

00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:21.046
can sustain while still meeting its requirements.

00:00:21.046 --> 00:00:25.290
Scaling up changes one machine; scaling out adds
more machines.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.389
Both are practical tools with different costs.

00:00:28.389 --> 00:00:35.058
The worker example explains why concurrency can help
during IO waits even without additional cores.

00:00:35.058 --> 00:00:41.054
Diagnose the bottleneck first, then predict whether
more CPU, memory, storage capacity or

00:00:41.054 --> 00:00:43.479
independent workers will address it.
